course: dictionary
lesson: 3774
title: Fondly: kind or foolish?
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words tell you how someone does something. «She smiled fondly at the baby.» The word fondly tells you how she smiled: kindly and with love. The smile is the action, and fondly describes the feeling in it.
explain: Look at the words around the word to find its meaning. Smiling at a baby, remembering a friend, touching a child's head: these are warm, loving actions. So fondly means in a kind, loving way.
explain: Fondly has a second meaning, and the clues show which one fits. «He fondly believed he would win.» Believed shows he hoped for a win, but the story suggests he was wrong to hope. Here fondly means hopefully but wrongly, or foolishly. Ask yourself: is the meaning kind, or is it foolish? Then swap your meaning into the sentence and check it still makes sense.
fact: Fondly is an adverb, so it tells you how something is done.
fact: «Fondly» means in a kind, loving way, as in «She smiled fondly at the baby».
fact: «Fondly» can also mean hopefully but wrongly, or foolishly, as in «He fondly believed he would win».
fact: «Sap» is Irish slang for a softie or a fool, and «Not bad, ya sap» is said fondly.
fact: «Cheeky monkey» means a naughty or rude child, and it is often said fondly (UK).
exercise: «She smiled fondly at the baby.» How did she smile? | Angrily and loudly | Quickly, to catch a bus | Sadly, in the rain | *Kindly and with love
exercise: «He fondly believed he would win.» What does fondly mean here? | *Hopefully but wrongly, foolishly | Quickly and loudly | Kindly and lovingly | Carefully and slowly
exercise: «They remember their grandad fondly.» Which word tells you how they remember him? | *Fondly | Loudly | Sadly | Quickly
test: «Dad looked fondly at the old photo of us.» What does fondly mean here? | Angrily | Very fast | *With warm, loving feelings | Without any care at all
test: «Lily fondly believed the bus would be on time.» The bus was late. What does fondly mean here? | Carefully | *Hopefully but wrongly, foolishly | Loudly | Kindly and gently
exam: «The old teacher spoke fondly of her first class.» What is the best meaning of fondly? | *Lovingly, with warm feelings | Sadly, with tears | Quietly, in a whisper | Angrily
